<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=medical-thermometer">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medical thermometer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1665">1665</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Clinical thermometer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A medical or clinical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermometer">thermometer</a> measures the body temperature of a human or other animal. It began as the water thermoscope that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a> built about 1592 to 1593, and Santorio Santorio put a measurable scale on it, writing of it in 1625. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> mathematician and physicist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens">Christiaan Huygens</a> created a clinical thermometer in 1665. Hermann Boerhaave and his students used it, and Anton de Haen concluded that a record of temperature could inform the doctor about a patient's health. In 1866 and 1867 Thomas Clifford Allbutt designed a portable version six inches long, and Carl Wunderlich in 1868 published over a million readings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medical-thermometer.jpg" alt="Medical thermometer" width="330" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacek Halicki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2023_Rtęciowy_termometr_lekarski.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
